Separating Memory-Traffic Effects from Runtime-Induced Energy Changes in Autoregressive LLM Decode: A Controlled-Intervention Framework

Ouadi Maakoul and SI TEAM

Abstract

This thesis asks whether the physical traffic generated by the KV-cache in autoregressive LLM decode can be separated from the runtime paths that co-occur with it. The central result is negative: duration is a post-treatment variable, and equal-duration comparisons impose no constraint on the power integral over the decode interval (Proposition 1). This is a mathematical non-identification observation; physical realizability is an empirical question, not a premise. The response is a partial-identification and intervention-validation architecture. Lemma 1 supplies a bookkeeping bound on the duration-associated integral under an explicit power envelope (Assumption A), enforced exogenously and explicitly scoped to the enforced regime rather than to natural DVFS. Treatment validity is reframed as a full instrumental-variable assumption set—independence, exclusion, relevance, and monotonicity (Condition 3)—with exclusion stated as a design-maintained assumption, because instrument validity is not testable without additional structural assumptions when the endogenous variable is continuous. Relevance is operationalised as a first-stage diagnostic against Stock–Yogo critical values, reported as a diagnostic rather than as a formal test. Monotonicity is stated as a design assumption with a source-level code-path audit, not as a hypothesis test. When Condition 3 holds, a Wald-type ratio estimates a local traffic response over the complier subpopulation; when it fails, the treatment is a bundle contrast and the ratio is not interpreted as a traffic effect. The architecture is a sequence of gates: instrument qualification, interventional specification, output equivalence, kernel-and-regime stability, traffic response, and measurement sufficiency. The framework is positioned as a causal validation tool for the KV coefficients of existing energy laws, with a pre-registered consistency criterion as its falsifiable hypothesis. A null or bundle outcome is reported as an identification limit, not as evidence for a hidden effect.

---

1. Problem Formulation

1.1 Energy as a functional of the realised power trajectory

Let P(t)\ge 0 denote instantaneous GPU-board power over a decoding interval [0,\tau]. The estimand throughout is board-level GPU energy. The restriction is not incidental: independent characterisation reports that GPU-only telemetry misses 41–45% of system energy across three LLM-agent workloads on a 2× RTX PRO 6000 Blackwell setup running Qwen3-8B/27B, with NVML GPU counters, Intel RAPL CPU/DRAM counters, and IPMI system-level sensors. That figure is specific to that hardware and workload and is not generalised. Measured energy is

E(\tau,P)=\int_{0}^{\tau}P(t)\,dt .

Write

P(t)=P_{\text{base}}\bigl(T(t),C(t)\bigr)+P_{\text{dyn}}\bigl(u(t)\bigr),

where T(t) is die temperature, C(t) the clock and power-management state, and u(t) the instantaneous execution activity—kernel mix, memory-controller occupancy, SM utilisation. The functional is not additive in \tau and u: both the integration limit and the integrand depend on the intervention.

Definition 1 (Intervention). An intervention Z=z assigns a treatment to the inference stack. The realised triple is

\bigl(K(z),\tau(z),P_z(\cdot)\bigr),

where K(z) is physical KV-cache traffic per generated token, reported separately as K_{\text{read}} and K_{\text{write}}; \tau(z) is decoding duration per token; and P_z(\cdot) is the realised power trajectory. The obstacle is that Z may act on all three coordinates simultaneously.

1.2 Causal objects

(A) Total intervention effect. Identified by randomisation of Z with a valid measurement protocol; not the effect of K alone:

\mathrm{TE}_Z=\mathbb{E}\bigl[E(Z=1)\bigr]-\mathbb{E}\bigl[E(Z=0)\bigr].

(B) Controlled direct effect (conceptual target, not an estimand).

\mathrm{CDE}(k_1,k_0;\tau_0,r_0)
=
\mathbb{E}\bigl[L(k_1,\tau_0,r_0)\bigr]
-
\mathbb{E}\bigl[L(k_0,\tau_0,r_0)\bigr],

where L(k,\tau,r) is the energy law under traffic k, duration \tau, and path r, with expectation over residual path randomness. The notation presumes an intervention setting K=k while holding \tau=\tau_0 and R=r_0 fixed. No such physical intervention has been validated; the CDE is a reference object only.

(C) Operationally identifiable effect.

\widehat{\Delta E}=\overline{E}_{Z=1}-\overline{E}_{Z=0},

with uncertainty from repeated-run variability and blocking, interpreted only under the stated identification assumptions.

1.3 Causal graph and the post-treatment problem

The provisional directed acyclic graph is

Z\to K\to E,\qquad Z\to K\to\tau\to E,\qquad R\to\tau,\qquad R\to P(t)\to E,

with X\to K,\tau,R,E for pre-treatment covariates. The intervention may also open

Z\to R\to E,\qquad Z\to\kappa\to P(t)\to E,

where \kappa denotes kernel-family identity. Both K and \tau are post-treatment variables. Conditioning on either in a regression

E_{\text{tok}}=\theta_0+\beta_K K+\beta_\tau\tau+\gamma_R R+\delta^\top X+\varepsilon

does not identify a direct effect; it induces collider stratification whenever K or \tau shares unmeasured causes with E—clock state, temperature, power limit, cache residency, memory-controller behaviour. Regression coefficients are therefore descriptive local responses, not causal parameters. This is consistent with the position that a cost estimand is not replaced by one conditioning on a post-treatment realised cost.

1.4 Logical versus physical KV traffic, with the decode regime stated correctly

FlashAttention's I/O-complexity analysis is a statement about the prefill regime, in which N queries attend to N keys. With d the head dimension and M the per-SM SRAM capacity,

\mathrm{IO}_{\mathrm{FA}}^{\text{prefill}}=O\!\left(\frac{N^2 d^2}{M}\right),

and this bound is optimal over a range of SRAM sizes; the analysis supplies both an upper bound and a matching lower bound showing that no exact attention algorithm can asymptotically improve on HBM accesses over all SRAM sizes. Standard attention instead materialises the N\times N score matrix, giving \Theta(N^2+Nd) HBM accesses.

Decode is a different regime. With a single query token N_q=1 and KV-cache length N, decode arithmetic intensity is approximately the batch size B in FLOP/byte, and charging KV reads lowers it further; decode throughput is set by HBM bandwidth rather than by SM compute. The dominant HBM traffic is the KV-cache read. The logical KV byte count per decode step is

L_{\text{KV,decode}}\approx 2 \, B \, N \, d \, b_{\text{KV}} \, n_{\text{layers}},

with b_{\text{KV}} bytes per KV element, factor 2 for K and V, and n_{\text{layers}} the number of transformer layers. Physical traffic K equals this logical count only if the kernel issues no redundant transactions (no padding loads, no over-fetch beyond the access pattern). The triple distinction is maintained:

L\ \text{(logical KV bytes)}
\neq
K\ \text{(physical KV traffic)}
\neq
K_{\text{total}}\ \text{(all HBM traffic)}.

Operationally, K_{\text{read}} and K_{\text{write}} are the Nsight Compute metrics dram__bytes_read.sum and dram__bytes_write.sum; K_{\text{total}} is their sum over the decode kernels. The prefill bound is not the relevant bound for decode, and citing it as if it governed decode traffic would conflate regimes.

1.5 Existing energy laws and the causal gap

The Token-Energy Law fits

e_{\text{gross},\sigma}
=
c_{\sigma}
+
\alpha_{\sigma}q_{\mathrm{w}}
+
\beta_{\sigma}q_{\mathrm{KV}}
+
P_{\tau,\sigma}\tau
+
u_{\sigma},

with q_{\mathrm{w}} and q_{\mathrm{KV}} logical weight and KV byte demands, \tau realised decode time, and per-configuration coefficients; held-out condition-mean estimates differ from measurement by at most 3.73%. The authors state that the coefficients "describe the effective responses to weight and KV demand" and "are effective responses rather than separately metered physical components". The constant KV-append contribution cannot be identified separately from the intercept through this regression.

The SIMPLE Law separates prefill from decode and splits decode into a KV-cache term and a next-token term, attaining R^2=0.969 and 11.97% MAPE across 13 dense LLMs on H100 without per-model calibration. Two figures must be kept distinct: more aggressive GQA KV grouping reduces per-token energy by up to 3\times relative to standard MHA; separately, the reported R^2 and MAPE correspond to accuracy that is 3.1–3.5\times below the SOTA. These are different quantities and are not interchangeable. The law also reports that prefix caching removes most prefill cost (-93\%) while the KV-cache term is unaffected.

Neither law claims causal identification of K. Both absorb traffic and runtime-path effects into fitted coefficients. The gap is precisely that: the laws parameterise KV cost, and the framework asks whether the parameter can be causally validated under an intervention that changes K without changing the runtime path.

