2. Identification Results

2.1 Proposition 1 (Non-identification of the CDE by duration matching)

Statement. Let Z\in\{0,1\} and suppose \tau(1)=\tau(0)=\tau_0 almost surely. Then equality of durations imposes no constraint on

E(1)-E(0)=\int_{0}^{\tau_0}\bigl(P_1(t)-P_0(t)\bigr)\,dt .

In particular, duration equality does not identify \mathrm{CDE}.

Proof. Energy under assignment z is E(z)=\int_0^{\tau_0}P_z(t)\,dt, well defined because board-level power is bounded between the platform limit and zero and is piecewise continuous in practice. Duration matching fixes only the upper limit. Write P_1(t)=P_0(t)+\delta(t); then

E(1)-E(0)=\int_0^{\tau_0}\delta(t)\,dt,

and \tau(1)=\tau(0)=\tau_0 places no restriction on \int_0^{\tau_0}\delta, which may be positive, negative, or zero. ∎

Remark on scope. The proposition is about the identifying power of the observable \tau: the event \{\tau(1)=\tau(0)=\tau_0\} is compatible with any value of the integral difference. It does not assert that two physically realisable executions with equal \tau and unequal E exist. That existence claim is a realizability lemma and is left as an empirical question.

Corollary (qualified form). Idle padding to equalise \tau is a well-defined intervention on the mediator when the padded interval is filled with a known idle state, and it point-identifies a controlled effect relative to idle-fill:

\Delta E_{\text{idle-fill}}
=
\int_0^{\tau_0}\bigl(P_1^{\text{active}}-P_0^{\text{idle}}\bigr)\,dt
+
\int_{\tau_0}^{\tau_1}P^{\text{idle}}(t)\,dt,

the second term being observed directly. This is not the CDE: the power on the padded interval is idle power, not the counterfactual active power that would have obtained under a duration-matched traffic reduction. Busy-waiting and dummy computation change u(t) and hence P_{\text{dyn}}; clock modification changes C(t) and hence both P_{\text{base}} and \tau. None yields the CDE without an additional power-path assumption.

2.2 Assumption A (Power envelope) and Lemma 1 (Bookkeeping bound)

Assumption A. Let \Delta\tau=\tau(1)-\tau(0)>0. The counterfactual power trajectory on the excess interval (\tau_0,\tau_1], under a fixed runtime path, satisfies

P_{\mathrm{lo}}\le P^{\text{cf}}(t)\le P_{\mathrm{hi}}
\qquad
\text{for }t\in(\tau_0,\tau_1].

The bounds are not estimated from the treatment arm's realised trajectory on the excess interval. They are fixed before measurement by one of: (i) an externally applied power cap P(t)\le P_{\mathrm{cap}}; (ii) clock locking at a frequency at which neither arm engages DVFS differently, with the envelope derived from the locked frequency and the observed activity range; (iii) a pilot-derived envelope reported with its derivation and treated as an assumption rather than a measurement. Assumption A is not testable from the main experiment alone.

Regime scope. The bound is on the duration integral under the enforced regime. Enforcing the envelope through power cap or clock lock changes C(t) and therefore the power trajectory itself. The bound does not transport to natural-DVFS production without an additional transport assumption, which this thesis does not make. Throughout, the bound is conditional on the enforced regime and is not automatically transferable to unconstrained deployment.

Lemma 1. Under Assumption A,

P_{\mathrm{lo}}\,\Delta\tau
\le
\int_{\tau_0}^{\tau_1}P^{\text{cf}}(t)\,dt
\le
P_{\mathrm{hi}}\,\Delta\tau .

Proof. Monotonicity of the integral over an interval of length \Delta\tau. ∎

The lemma concerns the counterfactual trajectory under a fixed runtime path, not the observed treatment-arm trajectory on the excess interval; this is precisely why Assumption A must be enforced exogenously.

Remark on decomposition dependence. The split

\Delta E
=
\underbrace{\int_0^{\tau_0}\bigl(P_1-P_0\bigr)}_{\text{path term}}
+
\underbrace{\int_{\tau_0}^{\tau_1}P_1}_{\text{duration term}}

is induced by the interval ordering; reversing the ordering yields a different decomposition. The bound is bookkeeping, not a causal decomposition, and does not separate a "duration effect" from a "path effect" in any causally unique sense.

2.3 Condition 3 (Intervention validity as a full IV assumption set)

Let \mathcal{G} denote execution-path attributes: kernel-family identity \kappa, runtime regime r, tensor shapes, and measured physical traffic K. For each run i, let K_i(1),K_i(0) be potential traffic and E_i(1),E_i(0) potential energy. A treatment Z is a valid instrument for K only if all four conditions below hold, with tolerances \epsilon_r,\epsilon,\delta_y pre-registered before measurement.

(C3-i) Independence. \{K_i(1),K_i(0),E_i(1),E_i(0)\}\perp\!\!\!\perp Z_i. Enforced by within-block randomisation.

(C3-ii) Exclusion. E_i(z)=E_i\bigl(K_i(z)\bigr) for z\in\{0,1\}; that is, Z affects E only through K. Operationally enforced by

\kappa_1=\kappa_0,
\qquad
\|r_1-r_0\|\le\epsilon_r,

with \kappa_1=\kappa_0 a discrete equality of kernel-family identity and \|r_1-r_0\|\le\epsilon_r a tolerance on continuous regime components. Failure of either relabels Z as a bundle contrast.

(C3-iii) Relevance. \Pr\bigl(K_i(1)\neq K_i(0)\bigr)>0, together with a pre-registered first-stage diagnostic. Let F_{\text{fs}} be the first-stage F-statistic from regressing K on Z within blocks. The design pre-registers a tolerable bias or size distortion and compares F_{\text{fs}} to the corresponding Stock–Yogo critical values—for a single endogenous regressor, 20.53 at 5% maximal relative bias, 11.46 at 10%, 6.65 at 20%. This is reported as a diagnostic, not as a formal Stock–Yogo test, because those tables are derived under linear IV with i.i.d. errors and the Cragg–Donald statistic, whereas the present ratio is of block-adjusted means. If F_{\text{fs}} falls below the pre-registered threshold, the Wald ratio is reported as weak-instrument-unstable and is not interpreted as a traffic response, even when |\widehat{\Delta K}| is nominally large. If F_{\text{fs}} is marginal, Anderson–Rubin inversion of the first-stage test is named as the correct weak-instrument inference and reported alongside the delta-method interval.

(C3-iv) Monotonicity. K_i(1)\ge K_i(0) for all i. The complier subpopulation is

\mathcal{C}=\{i: K_i(1)>K_i(0)\}.

Under (C3-i)–(C3-iv), the Wald-type ratio

\widehat{\beta}_{K}^{\text{Wald}}
=
\frac{\widehat{\Delta E}}{\widehat{\Delta K}}

estimates

\mathbb{E}\!\left[\frac{E_i(1)-E_i(0)}{K_i(1)-K_i(0)}\,\Big|\,i\in\mathcal{C}\right],

the average per-unit-traffic energy response over compliers, under the additional assumption that the individual-level ratio is constant over \mathcal{C} or that a weighted average over \mathcal{C} is the target. Without monotonicity, defiers are present and the ratio mixes complier and defier effects; the sign is then not interpretable without an additional weighting assumption.

Monotonicity as a design assumption, not a testable hypothesis. Because K is continuous, K_i(1) and K_i(0) are potential outcomes and only one is observed per run; a per-run diagnostic of \Delta K_i is not implementable. The Kitagawa and Mourifié–Wan monotonicity tests are derived for binary treatment with a discrete instrument and do not transfer to this setting. (C3-iv) is therefore stated as a design assumption whose plausibility is checked by a source-level code-path audit: indexing-based skipping can only remove loads that the padded baseline issues, so a mechanistic defier would require the skipping kernel to issue a redundant load the padded kernel does not issue. Such a path is a property of the kernel implementation to be audited in source, not a distribution to be plotted; if it exists, monotonicity fails by construction. If the weaker condition "more runs move in the compliant direction than in the defiant direction" is invoked, it is named explicitly and the resulting ratio is not called a LATE.

Non-testability of exclusion, with scope. Instrument validity is not testable without additional structural assumptions when the endogenous variable is continuously distributed. Gunsilius proves non-testability in that setting and shows that weak structural assumptions—continuity between observable variables—can re-establish theoretical testability, with continuity already sufficient and monotonicity adding further testable implications. This thesis does not invoke those structural assumptions and therefore treats (C3-ii) as design-maintained: enforced by the kernel-and-regime gate and probed by the sham controls, which are placebo tests of specific violations rather than a proof of exclusion. The gate architecture is not merely prudent; in the absence of the structural assumptions that would restore testability, it is the only available enforcement mechanism for Condition 3.

Relation to Gunsilius-type sharp bounds. The sharp-bounds result under no or minimal structural assumptions is a separate identification strategy, not a validation of the Wald ratio. If continuity is later invoked to obtain sharp bounds on the K\to E effect, the estimand and the inferential route both change, and the two must not be conflated.

2.4 Proposition 2 (Two-arm measurement consistency and traffic stationarity)

Let \mathcal{A} (unprofiled energy) and \mathcal{B} (profiled traffic) be the primary arms and \mathcal{B}' a diagnostic arm defined below. Cross-arm merging is valid only if

\|\tau_{\mathcal{A}}-\tau_{\mathcal{B}}\|\le\epsilon_\tau,
\qquad
\kappa_{\mathcal{A}}=\kappa_{\mathcal{B}},
\qquad
\|r_{\mathcal{A}}-r_{\mathcal{B}}\|\le\epsilon_r,
\qquad
\|\dot n_{\mathcal{A}}-\dot n_{\mathcal{B}}\|\le\epsilon_{\dot n},

with tolerances derived from pilot variance rather than chosen post hoc. If any condition fails, the traffic measurement is reported separately and is not merged with the energy estimate.

Nsight Compute by default flushes GPU caches before each replay pass so that counter values are deterministic; under default cache control, \mathcal{B} measures a cold-cache replay, not necessarily production decode. Define the stationarity ratio

\rho_z=\frac{K_{\text{profiled}}(z)}{L_{\text{KV}}(z)}.

The two-arm design assumes \rho_1/\rho_0\in[\underline{\rho},\overline{\rho}].

Arm \mathcal{B}' as a profiling-mode diagnostic. Cache control is independent of replay mode and must be disabled explicitly; --replay-mode application --cache-control none lets the application prime caches implicitly and better replicates application behaviour when cache priming is part of the measured workload. The design therefore adds

\mathcal{B}':
\quad\texttt{--replay-mode application --cache-control none},

and computes \rho_z^{\mathcal{B}'} alongside \rho_z^{\mathcal{B}}. The stationarity test becomes a comparison:

· if \rho_z^{\mathcal{B}'}/\rho_z^{\mathcal{B}} is stable across z and close to one, cold-cache bias is not driving \Delta K;
· if it diverges, both ratios are reported, the arms are not merged, and Gate 3 fails.

Caveat: disabling cache control can produce incorrect results for multi-pass metrics, so \mathcal{B}' is a diagnostic for K, not a replacement for \mathcal{B}. The stationarity pilot includes at least three treatment levels and stratifies by sequence length N and batch size B, with both bands derived from pilot variance. If the ratio is unstable, the traffic measurement cannot characterise the treatment and the traffic-response gate fails regardless of \|\Delta K\|.

