3. Experimental Architecture

3.1 Gate −1 (Instrument qualification)

The total-energy counter is accessed through nvmlDeviceGetTotalEnergyConsumption. Support is device-, driver-, and SKU-dependent and is not inferable from architecture generation; architecture labels are not admissible proxies. The only reliable check is a direct query, treating NVML_ERROR_NOT_SUPPORTED as a hard constraint for that device/driver combination.

Gate −1 requires: (i) a direct NVML query confirming support; (ii) validation against a known load with the discrepancy reported; (iii) the recorded counter resolution; (iv) an empirical measurement of the counter's actual update interval—how often the value changes under load—rather than an assumption of millijoule-unit resolution; and (v) a pilot estimate of the minimum decode-window length needed to exceed the resolution by a factor of at least ten and the update interval by a pre-registered multiplier.

Metrological floor. Cumulative-energy counters show frequent stale reads for intervals below approximately 100\,\mathrm{ms}, with a sampling floor measured at roughly 100\,\mathrm{ms} rather than 50\,\mathrm{ms}, and consumer-class devices under-report energy at sub-millisecond intervals by a large margin. Newer parts update at intervals reported in the 20–100 ms range. Reliability is moreover platform-stratified: workstation- and data-centre-class parts show closer agreement between cumulative energy and integrated power despite high stale-read rates, so the floor must be estimated per hardware class rather than applied uniformly.

Let r_{\text{res}} be the counter resolution in joules and \bar P the mean board power. The resolution-limited window is

\tau_{\text{res}}=\frac{10\,r_{\text{res}}}{\bar P},

the update-limited window is \tau_{\text{upd}}=k^{\text{pre}}_u\,\widehat{u}, where \widehat{u} is the estimated update interval and k^{\text{pre}}_u a pre-registered multiplier, and the gate requires

\tau_{\min}\ge\max\{\tau_{\text{res}},\tau_{\text{upd}}\}.

Fallback hierarchy. If support is absent, the study does not substitute NVML power polling at a rate faster than the device's documented update interval; doing so yields repeated reads of the same box-car value plus aliasing rather than higher resolution. It either restricts to supported hardware or uses an external power meter with a documented sampling rate and a pre-registered integration rule, calibrated against the counter on supported hardware in a pilot. Failure of (ii), (iv), or (v) yields a reported identification limit and no treatment definition.

3.2 Gate 0 (Interventional specification)

The treatment is defined at the kernel level. The primary candidate is indexing-based skipping: the attention kernel reads the block table and issues loads only for valid blocks, without loading padding and without branching into a different kernel family. Loaded-and-masked and multiply-by-zero padding increase physical traffic by loading padding that indexing would skip and are therefore sham controls, not primary treatments. The architectural rationale is that PagedAttention's advantage derives from not loading padding; its memory-waste advantage reaches up to 8.9\times at 112{,}000 tokens relative to standard baselines because contiguous allocation wastes 60–80% of VRAM through fragmentation while paging reduces the waste to under 4%. This is a memory-waste claim, not an energy-per-token claim: fragmentation enables throughput and does not by itself imply an energy reduction.

The SenseNova-U1 paged-decode implementation supplies a documented instance—an existence proof—of the masking penalty's magnitude. In vllm_omni.diffusion.models.sensenova_u1.paged_decode, with BLOCK_SIZE = 16 and BUCKETS = (512, 1024, 2048, 4096, 8192), masked bucket attention measured on an A800 at real decode shapes runs 7.96–11.55 ms per step against 1.03 ms for exact-length unmasked attention. The documentation states the resolution directly: padding the cache to a bucket and masking the tail gives static shapes, but the mask is what costs; the way out is a paged cache in which flash_attn_varlen_func takes the used length as a tensor alongside a block table, so buffers stay bucket-sized and capturable while the kernel reads only the valid prefix. This is cited as evidence that a padded-KV treatment can change \kappa and \tau substantially in at least one real implementation. It motivates Gate 2; it establishes no general ratio and does not substitute for Gate 2.

3.3 Gate 1 (Output equivalence)

Exact token match against the reference condition, or a pre-registered logit-deviation tolerance \delta_y. Failure terminates the treatment.

3.4 Gate 2 (Kernel and regime stability)

\kappa_1=\kappa_0 and \|r_1-r_0\|\le\epsilon_r with \epsilon_r pre-registered. Failure relabels Z as a bundle treatment; the Wald ratio is not reported as a traffic response. Because exclusion is not testable without structural assumptions when K is continuous, this gate is the primary enforcement of (C3-ii): a design condition, not a diagnostic.

3.5 Gate 3 (Physical-traffic response)

\|\Delta K\|\ge\epsilon, with K_{\text{read}} and K_{\text{write}} reported separately, the stationarity check of Proposition 2 passed including the \mathcal{B}' comparison, and the first-stage F_{\text{fs}} of (C3-iii) reported with the pre-registered threshold and the Anderson–Rubin inversion when F_{\text{fs}} is marginal.

3.6 Gate 4 (Measurement sufficiency and power)

A pre-registered MDE is computed from pilot variance. The block-level model is

\Delta E_b=\mu+\eta_b+\varepsilon_b,
\qquad
\eta_b\sim(0,\sigma_\eta^2),
\qquad
\varepsilon_b\sim(0,\sigma_\varepsilon^2),

with \mu=\Delta E the parameter of interest and \eta_b absorbing thermal and clock drift. For paired block differences of variance \sigma^2_\Delta,

\mathrm{MDE}
=
\bigl(z_{1-\alpha^{\text{pre}}/2}+z_{1-\beta^{\text{pre}}}\bigr)
\sqrt{\frac{2\,\sigma^2_\Delta}{B}}.

The variance of \widehat{\Delta E} is estimated by block-level resampling; the repeated reference condition tests for drift, and a large drift term triggers the thermal-clock gate rather than a post-hoc correction. If the MDE exceeds the expected effect size, the study reports an identification limit and terminates before the full experiment. Condition order is randomised within blocks and gate evaluation is blinded to treatment labels where the runtime permits; the blinding status is recorded per run.

3.7 Two-arm layout

Arm Purpose Profiler Primary quantity
\mathcal{A} Energy estimation Off Cumulative NVML energy difference per token
\mathcal{B} Traffic and kernel characterisation On, default cache control K_{\text{read}},K_{\text{write}}, kernel identity, regime
\mathcal{B}' Cache-control diagnostic On, --cache-control none \rho_z^{\mathcal{B}'} versus \rho_z^{\mathcal{B}}
\mathcal{A}' Profiler-on/off calibration On Cross-arm consistency check (Proposition 2)

Energy is measured with cumulative counters rather than power polling, because one-second power averages cannot resolve short decode windows or transitions. Long repeated windows increase the measured difference relative to both the resolution and the update interval.

3.8 Blocking, randomisation, and the thermal-clock gate

Conditions are randomised within blocks and a repeated reference condition is interleaved to detect drift. The measurement window opens only after a thermal-clock gate: die temperature variance and SM-clock variance must both fall below pre-registered thresholds over a pre-registered window, and no throttle events may occur during the window. Thermal equilibrium alone is insufficient, because decode can run at high SM frequency despite a warm die when power draw is low; the gate therefore records temperature, SM clock, power, and throttle reasons per run and opens only when both thermal and clock stability hold.

3.9 Positioning and the pre-registered law-consistency criterion

The novelty claim is restricted to KV-cache decode traffic under the stated intervention. Causal inference for configurable systems already treats configuration options as causes of code-path and hardware-component activation; that methodology is not claimed as new. Nor does the thesis replace the Token-Energy Law or the SIMPLE Law, both of which already parameterise KV cost—the former with coefficients explicitly described as effective responses rather than separately metered components, the latter with a four-parameter law attaining R^2=0.969 and 11.97% MAPE across 13 dense LLMs on H100 without per-model calibration. The gap is that these laws fit the KV coefficient and cannot distinguish a change in K from a change in the runtime path co-occurring with it.

Pre-registered consistency criterion. Let \hat{\beta}_{KV} be the fitted KV coefficient of the target law under the tested configuration and

\Delta E_{\text{pred}}
=
\hat{\beta}_{KV}\,\Delta K

the predicted energy change under a clean traffic intervention. The design pre-registers a tolerance band

T_{\text{law}}
=
\bigl[-\delta_{\text{law}},\,\delta_{\text{law}}\bigr],
\qquad
\delta_{\text{law}} \text{ derived from pilot variance and the declared MDE},

and the rule

\text{law supported}
\iff
\widehat{\Delta E}-\Delta E_{\text{pred}}\in T_{\text{law}},
\qquad
\text{law falsified}
\iff
\widehat{\Delta E}-\Delta E_{\text{pred}}\notin T_{\text{law}}.

If the observed difference lies outside T_{\text{law}}, the coefficient is reported as absorbing runtime-path effects—a substantive finding about the law, not a failure of the experiment. If Condition 3 fails, the consistency test is not run, because there is no clean traffic intervention to compare against.

