Appendix B — Proofs and algebraic derivations

B.1 Proposition 1 (restatement, with a measure-theoretic note)

The proof in §2.1 is elementary and is not repeated. Two points of precision are added.

(i) Integrability. The identity E(z)=\int_0^{\tau_0}P_z(t)\,dt presumes P_z is Lebesgue-integrable on [0,\tau_0]. Board-level power is bounded above by the platform power limit and below by zero, and is piecewise continuous in practice; the integral is therefore well defined. No further regularity is required.

(ii) What the proposition does and does not say. The proposition is a statement about the identifying power of the observable \tau: the event \{\tau(1)=\tau(0)=\tau_0\} is compatible with any value of \int_0^{\tau_0}(P_1-P_0)\,dt. It does not assert that two physically realisable executions with equal \tau and unequal E exist. That existence claim is a realizability lemma and is explicitly left as an empirical question in §2.1.

B.2 Lemma 1 (restatement)

Under Assumption A, P_{\mathrm{lo}}\le P^{\text{cf}}(t)\le P_{\mathrm{hi}} on (\tau_0,\tau_1] implies

P_{\mathrm{lo}}\,\Delta\tau
\;\le\;
\int_{\tau_0}^{\tau_1}P^{\text{cf}}(t)\,dt
\;\le\;
P_{\mathrm{hi}}\,\Delta\tau,
\qquad \Delta\tau=\tau_1-\tau_0.

This is monotonicity of the integral. The lemma concerns the counterfactual trajectory under a fixed runtime path; it is not a statement about the observed treatment-arm trajectory on the excess interval, which is why Assumption A must be enforced exogenously (§2.2).

B.3 Corollary (idle-fill), derivation

Let the padded arm run active work on [0,\tau_0] and idle on (\tau_0,\tau_1], and the reference arm run active work on [0,\tau_0] only. Then

E_{\text{pad}}-E_{\text{ref}}
=
\int_0^{\tau_0}\bigl(P^{\text{active}}_1-P^{\text{active}}_0\bigr)\,dt
+
\int_{\tau_0}^{\tau_1}P^{\text{idle}}(t)\,dt .

The second term is observed directly by measuring the counter increment over the padded interval. This identifies a controlled effect relative to idle-fill, not the CDE, because the counterfactual power on (\tau_0,\tau_1] under a duration-matched traffic reduction would be P^{\text{active}}, not P^{\text{idle}}. No algebraic rearrangement recovers the CDE without an additional power-path assumption.

B.4 Wald ratio with block adjustment

Let blocks be b=1,\dots,B, conditions z\in\{0,1\}, and let \bar E_{b,z}, \bar K_{b,z} denote within-block condition means. Define

\widehat{\Delta E}=\frac{1}{B}\sum_{b=1}^{B}\bigl(\bar E_{b,1}-\bar E_{b,0}\bigr),
\qquad
\widehat{\Delta K}=\frac{1}{B}\sum_{b=1}^{B}\bigl(\bar K_{b,1}-\bar K_{b,0}\bigr).

The Wald ratio is

\widehat{\beta}^{\text{Wald}}_{K}=\frac{\widehat{\Delta E}}{\widehat{\Delta K}}.

The primary uncertainty estimator is block-level resampling: resample blocks with replacement, recompute \widehat{\Delta E}^{(r)} and \widehat{\Delta K}^{(r)}, and report percentile intervals. A delta-method cross-check is

\widehat{\mathrm{Var}}\bigl(\widehat{\beta}^{\text{Wald}}_{K}\bigr)
\approx
\frac{1}{\widehat{\Delta K}^{\,2}}
\Bigl[
\widehat{\mathrm{Var}}(\widehat{\Delta E})
+
\widehat{\beta}^{\,2}\,\widehat{\mathrm{Var}}(\widehat{\Delta K})
-
2\widehat{\beta}\,\widehat{\mathrm{Cov}}(\widehat{\Delta E},\widehat{\Delta K})
\Bigr],

with \widehat{\beta}=\widehat{\beta}^{\text{Wald}}_{K}. The delta-method interval is a cross-check, not the primary interval; when F_{\text{fs}} is marginal, Anderson–Rubin inversion is reported in addition (§4.2) and the delta-method interval is flagged as unreliable.

Honest caveat. The ratio \widehat{\Delta E}/\widehat{\Delta K} has a heavy-tailed sampling distribution when \widehat{\Delta K} is near zero. The first-stage diagnostic (C3-iii) exists precisely to pre-empt reporting a ratio whose denominator is too small to support inference.

B.5 Residual interval algebra

With B_{\mathrm{lo}}=P_{\mathrm{lo}}\Delta\tau and B_{\mathrm{hi}}=P_{\mathrm{hi}}\Delta\tau, the residual interval is

R=\bigl[\widehat{\Delta E}-B_{\mathrm{hi}},\ \widehat{\Delta E}-B_{\mathrm{lo}}\bigr].

Its width is

|R|=(P_{\mathrm{hi}}-P_{\mathrm{lo}})\,\Delta\tau,

independent of \widehat{\Delta E}. The interval excludes zero if and only if

\widehat{\Delta E}>B_{\mathrm{hi}}
\quad\text{or}\quad
\widehat{\Delta E}<B_{\mathrm{lo}}.

The label duration_envelope_incompatible (Algorithm 3) is a statement about the envelope, not about causality: it says the duration envelope cannot account for the observed total effect. It does not say the residual is a KV effect. Path-term contributions, thermal drift, clock variation, and measurement error remain in R by construction.

