Appendix C — Condition 3 stated formally

C.1 Potential outcomes

For each run i, let K_i(1),K_i(0) be potential physical traffic and E_i(1),E_i(0) potential energy. Only one of each pair is observed, according to Z_i.

C.2 The four assumptions

(C3-i) Independence. \{K_i(1),K_i(0),E_i(1),E_i(0)\}\perp\!\!\!\perp Z_i. Enforced by within-block randomisation (§3.8).

(C3-ii) Exclusion. E_i(z)=E_i\bigl(K_i(z)\bigr) for z\in\{0,1\}; i.e. Z affects E only through K. Operationally enforced by \kappa_1=\kappa_0 and \|r_1-r_0\|\le\epsilon^{\text{pre}}_r.

(C3-iii) Relevance. \Pr\bigl(K_i(1)\neq K_i(0)\bigr)>0, together with a first-stage diagnostic F_{\text{fs}}\ge F^{\text{pre}}_{\text{thr}}. For a single endogenous regressor, the Stock–Yogo critical values are 20.53 at 5% maximal relative bias, 11.46 at 10%, and 6.65 at 20%. These are reported as a diagnostic, not a formal test, because the tables are derived under linear IV with i.i.d. errors and the Cragg–Donald statistic, whereas the present ratio is of block-adjusted means.

(C3-iv) Monotonicity. K_i(1)\ge K_i(0) for all i.

C.3 What the Wald ratio identifies under all four

Under (C3-i)–(C3-iv), the complier subpopulation is \mathcal{C}=\{i:K_i(1)>K_i(0)\}, and

\frac{\mathbb{E}[E\mid Z=1]-\mathbb{E}[E\mid Z=0]}
     {\mathbb{E}[K\mid Z=1]-\mathbb{E}[K\mid Z=0]}
=
\mathbb{E}\!\left[\frac{E_i(1)-E_i(0)}{K_i(1)-K_i(0)}\,\Big|\,i\in\mathcal{C}\right],

the average per-unit-traffic energy response over compliers, under the additional assumption that the individual-level ratio is constant over \mathcal{C} or that a weighted average over \mathcal{C} is the target. This is the standard LATE-type identity restated for continuous treatment; it is not a new result.

C.4 What fails when each assumption fails

Failing assumption Consequence for \widehat{\beta}^{\text{Wald}}_{K}
(C3-i) Independence Ratio confounded by assignment-correlated path determinants
(C3-ii) Exclusion Ratio is a bundle contrast; not a traffic response
(C3-iii) Relevance Ratio unstable; delta-method interval unreliable
(C3-iv) Monotonicity Ratio mixes complier and defier effects; sign not interpretable without an additional weighting assumption

The design relabels a treatment as a bundle when (C3-ii) fails and as weak-instrument-unstable when (C3-iii) fails. Both labels are substantive results, not nuisance outcomes.

