Appendix D — Non-testability: precise statement and scope

D.1 The statement used in this thesis

Instrument validity (C3-ii) is not testable without additional structural assumptions when the endogenous variable K is continuously distributed. This is the qualifier used in §2.3, §3.4, §4.4, and §5.2. The unqualified form (“not testable”) is not used. Gunsilius proves non-testability of instrument validity in the continuous-treatment setting.

D.2 What continuity would buy

Gunsilius shows that weak structural assumptions on the model—continuity between observable variables—can re-establish theoretical testability in the continuous setting. This thesis does not invoke continuity or monotonicity as testable restrictions on the data. It therefore treats (C3-ii) as design-maintained.

D.3 Why the thesis does not invoke continuity

Invoking continuity would convert the design into a different identification strategy (sharp bounds under weak structural assumptions), not a validation of the Wald ratio. The two strategies have different estimands, different inferential guarantees, and different failure modes. Conflating them would misrepresent what the gate architecture delivers.

D.4 Relation to Gunsilius-type sharp bounds

If a future version adopts the continuity route, the deliverable becomes a sharp-bound interval on the K\to E effect under minimal structural assumptions, and the Wald ratio is not the object of inference. The present thesis records this as a forward path, not a result.

