Appendix E — Instrument qualification protocol (Gate −1)

E.1 Counter support query

The only admissible test for counter support is a direct call to nvmlDeviceGetTotalEnergyConsumption on the target device and driver. A non-success return is treated as a hard constraint for that device/driver combination. Architecture generation is not used as a proxy; support is device-, driver-, and SKU-dependent. NVIDIA documents the counter for Volta and newer devices, but support must be queried per device.

E.2 Known-load validation

Run a fixed, repeatable load of known duration and compare the counter increment to an independent measurement (e.g. an external meter in a pilot, or a manufacturer-specified power profile). Report the discrepancy and its direction. A systematic bias is a finding, not a nuisance; it must be reported before the main experiment.

E.3 Update-interval estimation

Let C(t_k) be counter reads at sampling times t_k=t_0+k\delta, with \delta chosen well below the expected update interval. Define the indicator of change

X_k=\mathbf{1}\{C(t_{k+1})\neq C(t_k)\}.

The empirical update interval is estimated as

\widehat{u}
=
\frac{1}{\sum_k X_k}\sum_{k:\,X_k=1}(t_{k+1}-t_k),

the mean spacing between observed counter changes, reported with a bootstrap interval. This replaces the assumption of millijoule-unit resolution with a measured quantity.

E.4 Stale-read test and platform stratification

Cumulative-energy counters show frequent stale reads for intervals below approximately 100\,\mathrm{ms}, with strong platform-dependent behavior: consumer-class GPUs can under-report energy by 95.4% at 0.5 ms relative to integrated power measurements, while workstation- and data-centre-class parts show closer agreement despite high stale-read rates. Newer GPUs update readings every 20–100 ms. Gate −1 therefore reports (a) the estimated update interval \widehat{u} stratified by hardware class, and (b) the smallest window length at which the counter increment is reproducible across repeated identical runs within a pre-registered coefficient of variation.

E.5 Minimum window length

Let r_{\text{res}} be the counter resolution in joules and \bar P the mean board power. The resolution-limited window is

\tau_{\text{res}}=\frac{10\,r_{\text{res}}}{\bar P},

the update-limited window is

\tau_{\text{upd}}=k^{\text{pre}}_u\,\widehat{u},

with k^{\text{pre}}_u a pre-registered multiplier. Gate −1 requires decode windows of length

\tau_{\min}\ge\max\{\tau_{\text{res}},\tau_{\text{upd}}\}.

Both quantities are reported; neither is assumed.

E.6 External-meter fallback

If the counter is unsupported, the thesis does not substitute NVML power polling at a rate faster than the documented update interval. It either restricts to supported hardware or uses an external power meter with a documented sampling rate and a pre-registered integration rule, calibrated against the counter on supported hardware in a pilot. The calibration discrepancy is reported.

