Appendix J — Blocking, randomisation, and power

J.1 Block model

\Delta E_b=\mu+\eta_b+\varepsilon_b,
\qquad
\eta_b\sim(0,\sigma^2_\eta),
\qquad
\varepsilon_b\sim(0,\sigma^2_\varepsilon).

The parameter of interest is \mu=\Delta E. The block random effect absorbs thermal and clock drift. The reference condition is interleaved within each block to test for drift; a significant drift term is reported and, if large, triggers the thermal-clock gate (§3.8) rather than a post-hoc correction.

J.2 Randomisation

Condition order is randomised within blocks. Gate evaluation is blinded to treatment labels where the runtime permits; where blinding is not possible, the gate evaluator records the blinding status per run.

J.3 MDE

For paired block differences with variance \sigma^2_\Delta, the minimum detectable effect at significance \alpha^{\text{pre}} and power 1-\beta^{\text{pre}} is

\mathrm{MDE}
=
\bigl(z_{1-\alpha^{\text{pre}}/2}+z_{1-\beta^{\text{pre}}}\bigr)
\sqrt{\frac{2\,\sigma^2_\Delta}{B}},

with z the standard normal quantile. If \mathrm{MDE} exceeds the expected effect size, the study reports an identification limit and terminates before the full experiment (§3.6).

