Appendix L — What is not claimed

1. No point-identified CDE. The CDE is a reference object, not an estimand.
2. No causal coefficient from a regression on K or \tau. Both are post-treatment.
3. No universal joules-per-byte coefficient. The dummy-traffic test returns a binary detection verdict, not a coefficient.
4. No LATE without (C3-i)–(C3-iv). If any fails, the ratio is relabelled.
5. No statistical test of monotonicity. (C3-iv) is a design assumption with a source-level audit.
6. No formal Stock–Yogo test. The first-stage F is a diagnostic, because the tables are derived under linear IV with i.i.d. errors.
7. No Imbens–Manski or Stoye coverage. The intervals are reported as identification and sampling intervals separately unless the procedure is fully implemented; the simplest Imbens–Manski interval lacks uniform coverage, and Stoye's modification addresses this by pre-testing whether the population bounds are sufficiently close.
8. No test of exclusion with continuous K. (C3-ii) is design-maintained; the sham controls falsify specific violations but do not prove exclusion.
9. No transport of the Assumption A bound to natural-DVFS production. The bound is regime-conditional.
10. No generalisation of the Blackwell 41–45% figure. It is specific to the hardware and workload reported in §1.1.
11. No general ratio from the SenseNova-U1 instance. It is a documented instance motivating Gate 2.
12. No claim that the residual interval is a KV effect. It is a bookkeeping residual under Assumption A.
13. No claim that the Token-Energy Law or the SIMPLE Law is “merely accounting.” The framework is a validation tool, not a replacement.

